  "details": "Here's how it's done successfully. Adding a feature for VRC+ shaders that are quest compatible and custom by offering a shader builder with various features like outlines, scrolling textures, etc with various limitations for quest. \n\nLimitations for quest would be a limit on the number of components and stuff like that.\n\nYour game will do a brief scan before loading an avatar to scan the shader for compatible components. The moment it finds something incompatible, it loads the fallback shader.\n\nThese VRC+ custom shaders work when you have custom shaders disabled, allowing people to protect themselves from shader crashers entirely.\n\nPeople with vrc+ who make avatars with vrc+ shaders will have their shaders shown for anyone as long as it's compatible with their system.\n\nPeople who don't have VRC+ who upload VRC+ shaders will only have their shaders shown by default to people who have turned on custom shaders on pc, and anyone with VRC+.\n\nThis means, the only way for quest users to see these fancy shaders (Even though quest VRC+ shaders are limited to 3 or 4 components like matcap, emission, and outline) would be to have VRC+ or hope the uploader has VRC+\n\nIn this way you will both encourage quest users and avatar creators to get VRC+ and simultaneously be offering a feature that pretty much abolishes shader crash avatars entirely",
